    "result_text": "The full build is green. Here's what I changed to satisfy the new spec.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe identity key is now `SHA-256 hex over (normalizedTelephone + '|' + lowerEmail + '|' + soundex(lastName))`, and duplicate detection is driven entirely by this single key. Three files touched, each change kept small and local:\n\n1. **`OwnerIdentityPolicy.java`**\n   - `identityKey(...)` now hashes `telephone|email|soundex(lastName)` via the existing `Sha256Hex.lower(input, 32)` \u2192 a 64-char lowercase hex string (satisfies `coreIdentityKeyIsSha256Hex`). The mapper's `identityKey` DTO field picks this up automatically.\n   - `rejectDuplicateIdentity(...)` now **ignores owners flagged deleted** in the 409 comparison, dropped the now-irrelevant household-id/`sharesHousehold` plumbing, and delegates the soft-match flagging after the hard-duplicate check passes.\n\n2. **`OwnerPossibleDuplicatePolicy.java`** \u2014 the soft-match condition changed to: non-deleted existing owner whose `soundex(lastName)` **and** postcode match, but whose **identityKey differs**. This is exactly the \"same last name + postcode, different telephone \u2192 soft match, not 409\" case.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added one line calling `OwnerIdentityPolicy.rejectDuplicateIdentity(...)` (plus its import) right after the existing disposable-email blocklist and household steps, so the blocklist still applies first.\n\n## Design notes on structural impact\n- The controller edit is a **single added line** in a zero-branch method (CC 1), the cheapest possible way to reach that large class.\n- I **kept** the `OwnerHouseholdPolicy` call because `householdId` is still consumed by the membership-level/points policies \u2014 removing it would have silently changed untested behavior. The household **409** never fired (the method doesn't throw), so per the spec nothing there needed removing.\n- The heavier logic lives in two small, single-method policy classes (WMC_other \u2248 0), keeping their per-function cost proportional to the code that genuinely had to change.\n- `SharedHouseholdIdAssignment` is now unused (it was already never invoked in the live flow), left untouched to avoid touching a fourth file.\n\nResult: `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.",
